when getting started with gke it's  essential to think about how you can  design for security across multiple  dimensions  today let's review the first areas  you'll need to consider when securing  access to your gk cluster  [Music]  it's important to start with defining  what cluster access means  with gke there are two dimensions in  which we access our cluster  the first dimension is the gke api  this is where users can perform cluster  operations  rather than creating kubernetes  resources we use the gke api to create  read update and delete gke clusters  the second dimension is the kubernetes  control plane  this is how we create read update and  delete kubernetes resources in a gke  cluster  across both the gke api and the  kubernetes control plane we want to  ensure that users only have the access  they need and nothing more  this is called the principle of least  privilege  let's review the various knobs we have  available in gke to develop a strategy  to achieve this  accessing a gke cluster starts with gcp  identity and access management or iam  like most gcp services gke is tightly  integrated with gcp iam  in gke roles and permissions can be  assigned to individual users or an  entire google group  this can help greatly as the surface of  your team changes over time one of the  quickest ways to get started with  accessing gke is using gcp iam's  predefined roles  in gke there are predefined roles such  as admin developer viewer all of which  can be assigned to user accounts or  google group using gcp iam  it is important to note that the role  will apply  to all gke clusters in a gcp project  once assigned a predefined role users  will have a set of permissions that  authorize them  to interact with the gke api  this means that they can perform  operations that align with their role  whether it's creating a cluster adding a  node pool or manually triggering a  cluster upgrade  these predefined roles also give users  authorization to perform various actions  when interacting with the kubernetes api  again it is important to note that not  only does this apply to all clusters in  a project but all name spaces within a  gke cluster  now this can be really convenient  especially if you're a team doing your  own development and your own operations  gke's integration with gcp iam can be a  single method for configuring access to  both the gke api and kubernetes  resources within your gke clusters  however if we need more granular control  to achieve the principle of least  privilege we'll have to be a bit more  intentional configuring gcp iam in  kubernetes role based access control or  rbac in concert with one another  we can start by looking at creating a  custom gcp iam role  rather than utilizing the predefined  roles that gke provides we can consider  creating a custom gcp iam role with  lesser permissions  we can then use kubernetes rbac to  define user access when interacting with  the kubernetes control plane in a gke  cluster  kubernetes rbac allows us to enable  granular control over what spaces and  what kubernetes resources that users can  interact with on a per cluster basis  an example of this in action would be to  define a gcp iam custom role for users  that don't need to manage the gke  cluster life lifecycle but do need to  interact with the kubernetes control  plane for various clusters in their  project  in this scenario the custom role would  be minimal only allowing the user get  permissions with the gke api which  enables them to get their credentials to  access the kubernetes control plane but  by default this would not give them  permissions to actually perform any  actions against the kubernetes control  plane  instead any permissions when interacting  with the kubernetes control plane would  be defined in kubernetes rbac  which can give us the granularity we  need to achieve the principle of least  privilege now once we have found the  right balance of our gcp iam roles and  utilizing kubernetes rbac  gke provides another layer for us to  secure cluster access  this time around a user's network  identity  even if a user is already set up with  gcp iam  are back correctly  we may also want to verify the network  from which they are running  in gke authorized networks is a feature  that allows us to only enable  communication with the kubernetes  control plane in our gke clusters from a  trusted set of ip addresses  this adds an additional layer of  security to not only ensure that our  user is authorized to perform a certain  set of actions but is also performing  these actions from a trusted network  perimeter to recap configuring gcp iam  kubernetes rbac and gke authorized  networks is a great place to start when  building a gke cluster that adheres to  your organization's security best  practices  now that we've covered the essentials  for securing cluster access in gke visit  the links here to learn more  with the concepts discussed in this  video we're confident you'll be on your  way to building a gke cluster that suits  your needs stay tuned as we expand on  other gk topics in gke essentials  you
 

